INT. PRIVATE LIBRARY OF A VICTORIAN MANOR - NIGHT

Candles gutter. A thunderclap rattles the leaded glass. In the center of the room, a body lies under a red velvet curtain, only a pair of polished shoes showing.

INSPECTOR PHILOMENA CRABTREE (50s, damp mackintosh, sharp eyes) enters, followed by CONSTABLE REGINALD OKAFOR-WELLS (20s, helmet under his arm). A third figure waits by the fireplace: the manor's housekeeper, MRS. GWENDOLEN SLOANE (60s, black dress, key ring at her waist).

CRABTREE
Who found him?

SLOANE
I did, Inspector. At half past nine.

CRABTREE
And the door?

SLOANE
Locked from within.

OKAFOR-WELLS
Then how did you get in?

SLOANE
I have a key.

CRABTREE
(to Okafor-Wells)
Constable, the windows.

He checks each in turn. Every latch is down.

OKAFOR-WELLS
Sealed, ma'am.

Crabtree kneels, lifts the curtain, and studies the dead man's face. A tiny smile crosses hers.

CRABTREE
Mrs. Sloane. Did Lord Ashworth take his evening port?

SLOANE
He did.

CRABTREE
From this decanter?

She taps a crystal vessel on the mantel. It is empty.

SLOANE
It was full when I left him.

CRABTREE
Constable, the glass.

Okafor-Wells lifts it with a handkerchief and sniffs. He recoils.

OKAFOR-WELLS
Almonds.

Mrs. Sloane's hand tightens on her keys. Crabtree rises slowly.

CRABTREE
Mrs. Sloane, I don't believe this man was alone when he died.

SLOANE
Inspector, I assure you--

A thump from the bookcase. All three freeze. The shelf swings inward on a hidden hinge and a gust of cold air blows out every candle but one.

Crabtree and Okafor-Wells spin toward the opening. Mrs. Sloane bolts for the door, but Okafor-Wells catches her sleeve and the two of them struggle, keys clattering across the floor.

CRABTREE
(calmly)
Constable, hold her.

OKAFOR-WELLS
I've got her, ma'am.

Crabtree raises the last candle toward the dark passage.

CRABTREE
(to the dark)
You may come out now.
